 words per INV-026)\\n3. **Romance heat level** (optional: sweet / warm / steamy / explicit; default warm)\\n4. **Fantasy subgenre preference** (optional: epic / urban / portal / dark / cozy; default epic)\\n\\n### Step 2: Gather Optional Enrichment\\n\\nIf the user provides more than just a premise, capture:\\n- **Style samples**: Reference fiction titles or passages for stylistic analysis (emphasize INV-023/INV-024 — transformative influence only, zero plagiarism)\\n- **Mood/tone preferences**: Overall emotional register (dark, whimsical, bittersweet, etc.)\\n- **Character sketches**: Any existing character concepts the user wants included\\n- **World fragments**: Pre-existing worldbuilding the user has in mind\\n- **Constraints**: Things to avoid, required tropes, series position (standalone, book N of M)\\n\\n### Step 3: Validate Inputs\\n\\nBefore proceeding, verify:\\n1. The story idea contains enough material to generate a romantic fantasy (must imply both a fantasy element AND a romantic relationship per INV-001)\\n2. If style samples are provided, confirm they are for abstract stylistic analysis only — not reproduction (INV-023, INV-024)\\n3. Word count is in reasonable range (40,000–200,000 words)\\n4. No conflicting constraints (e.g., \\\"no magic\\\" in a fantasy story)\\n\\nIf validation fails, explain what's missing and ask the user to clarify.\\n\\n### Step 4: Present Configuration Summary\\n\\nDisplay a clear summary of the story configuration:\\n```\\nStory Premise: {premise}\\nTarget Word Count: {count}\\nHeat Level: {level}\\nFantasy Subgenre: {subgenre}\\nMood/Tone: {tone}\\nStyle References: {list or \\\"none\\\"}\\nCharacter Seeds: {list or \\\"none\\\"}\\nWorld Seeds: {list or \\\"none\\\"}\\nConstraints: {list or \\\"none\\\"}\\n```\\n\\nAsk the user to confirm or modify before proceeding (INV-080).\\n\\n### Step 5: Write story-config.json\\n\\nOnce confirmed, write `story-config.json` with all gathered parameters including:\\n- `storyId`: Generated unique identifier\\n- `premise`: The user's story idea\\n- `targetWordCount`: Confirmed word count\\n- `heatLevel`: Romance heat level\\n- `fantasySubgenre`: Chosen subgenre\\n- `mood`: Tone preferences\\n- `styleSamples`: Array of reference materials (empty if none)\\n- `characterSeeds`: Array of character concepts (empty if none)\\n- `worldSeeds`: Array of world fragments (empty if none)\\n- `constraints`: Array of constraints (empty if none)\\n- `confirmedAt`: ISO 8601 timestamp of user confirmation\\n\\n### Step 6: Launch Pipeline\\n\\nDispatch `romantic-fantasy-writer` (the orchestrator) to begin autonomous production.\\n\\n### Step 7: Present Results\\n\\nAfter the orchestrator completes, read `delivery-report.json` and present to the user:\\n- Total word count and chapter count\\n- Quality metrics summary\\n- Any advisory notes from beta reading\\n- Links to the final manuscript files\\n\\n## Artifact Assignments\\n\\n**Reads:** delivery-report.json\\n**Writes:** story-config.json\\n\\n## Result Codes\\n\\nThe guide has no formal result codes — it is the user-facing entry point.\\n\\n## Status Contract\\n\\nThe guide does not write a status.json — it is invoked directly by the user and reports results conversationally.\\nAGENT_EOF\\n\\necho \\\"Guide written: $(wc -w \\\"$AGENTS_DIR/romantic-fantasy-writer-guide.agent.md\\\")\\\"\", \"description\": \"Write guide agent\", \"initial_wait\": 10}"
